 "notes": "Hidden photon (dark photon) kinetic mixing chi vs mass m_gamma'. Headline measured bound is the 2s_{1/2}-2p_{1/2} Lamb-shift limit in atomic hydrogen (blue curve, Fig. 3), stated as one of 'the best ones we can derive from atomic spectra'. Curve computed analytically from Eq. (3.8), |dE| = chi^2 * alpha * a * m^2 / [2(1+a m)^4] with Bohr radius a=1/(alpha m_e), set equal to the conservative 2-sigma uncertainty DeltaM* = 2e-10 eV (Fig. 3 caption). Verified against the vector figure: minimum chi~1.5e-5 near m~3.7 keV, reaching chi=1 at m~1 GeV. Excluded region is above the U-shaped curve; both branches reported. The nearly-coincident green curve (combined 1s-2s + 2s-8s, correctly renormalised alpha) gives essentially the same bound within <factor 2. The hydrogen-like helium (Z=2) bound in Fig. 4 reaches slightly further but is presented as a Z-trend illustration. Not a dark-matter search, so no density dependence. Perturbation theory breaks down for chi ~ O(1); curve truncated at chi=1.",
